{{$NEXT}}
  - Breaking: heading_path is the chain of a section's ancestors only. With
    "# Intro", "## Alpha", "## Beta", Beta is now Intro#Beta (was
    Intro#Alpha#Beta) and a following "# Other" is Other (was Intro#Other),
    so a section's address no longer changes when a sibling is inserted
    before it. Addresses stored from 0.001 must be fetched anew via get_toc.
  - Breaking: headings that share a heading_path are all reachable. The
    second and later one gets a suffix on its last segment ("Page#Notes[2]",
    "[3]", ...) in get_toc and in every tool, the history tools included;
    the first keeps its address. Before, the later ones were unreachable.
  - Breaking: "\" and "#" inside a heading's text are written "\\" and "\#"
    in a heading_path, and a text ending in "[digits]" has that "[" written
    "\[", so the heading "A#B" and the pair "A" > "B" no longer share an
    address. MCP::Wiki::TOC::Parser gets escape_segment and
    split_heading_path for the two directions. A closing run of # ends a
    heading's text only after whitespace: "# C#" is "C#" (was "C").
  - Breaking: headings are recognised by CommonMark's rules. Fenced code
    blocks of ``` and ~~~, a leading YAML front matter block, HTML comment
    blocks and lines indented by four or more spaces no longer yield
    headings, so the addresses of the sections below them change; ATX
    headings indented by one to three spaces are now headings. Setext
    headings stay unsupported. A page that starts with a UTF-8 byte order
    mark keeps its first heading (the mark is ignored when parsing and stays
    in the file), so its sections get their real addresses ("Title#Sub", not
    "Sub").
  - Breaking: get_toc anchors follow GitHub: letters and digits of every
    script are kept ("Über Größe" is "über-größe", was "ber-gr-e"; "日本語"
    is no longer empty), other punctuation is dropped, each space becomes a
    hyphen ("A & B" is "a--b", was "a-b"), and equal anchors on a page get
    -1, -2, ...
  - Breaking: a section's text is its body without the heading line, in
    get_paragraph and update_paragraph alike. update_paragraph replaces the
    body only and leaves the heading and the following section untouched
    (it used to swallow the first line of the next section); content that
    still starts with the heading line now gets that heading twice. Writing
    back what get_paragraph returned leaves the page byte for byte as it
    was, front matter and closing fences included.
  - Breaking: update_paragraph refuses content that changes the structure of
    the page: a heading line, or a code fence or HTML comment left open
    ("# X" in a body used to re-parent the sections after it, an unclosed
    fence hid them). Sections are added with the new add_section.
  - Breaking: every tool is confined to the wiki root given at start
    (wiki_root / --wiki-root). The root_dir tool argument is gone and a call
    that passes one is refused; page names are resolved (symlinks, "..") and
    must lie below the root. A page is a .md file: any other file, a
    directory, FIFO or device named like a page, and everything below a .git
    directory are refused. list_pages returns exactly the pages the other
    tools accept.
  - Breaking: rename_page and delete_page refuse a page name that is itself
    a symlink (also as the target of a rename); they used to act on the page
    the link points to and leave the link dangling. Name that page instead.
    Reading and writing through a link work as before.
  - Breaking (Docker): the container always runs as uid 1000 (or the --user
    given) and never changes the ownership of a mounted directory; it used
    to run as the owner of the mount and to hand a root-owned volume to uid
    1000. A wiki root that is missing or not writable for that uid stops the
    container with a message that says how to fix it (--user, or write
    access).
  - New tools to change a page's structure: add_section (below a parent or
    in front of a sibling, answers with the new heading_path),
    delete_section, rename_heading and write_page (replaces a whole existing
    page). They take an optional reason and, where they change existing
    text, expected_hash. The tool count is now 15.
  - New tool search_pages: case-insensitive plain-text search over the pages
    (optionally below a directory), with the section, line and a snippet of
    each match, bounded by limit.
  - New tool get_page returns the whole page and its hash. The text before
    the first heading, front matter included, is read and written with the
    heading_path "" in get_paragraph and update_paragraph; a page without
    any heading is all preamble.
  - Every section has a hash, the SHA-256 of its bytes on disk, shown by
    get_toc and get_paragraph. update_paragraph takes an optional
    expected_hash and refuses, writing nothing, when the section changed
    since it was read, so two clients no longer overwrite each other
    unseen; the answer carries the new hash.
  - list_pages takes an optional prefix, a directory, and walks only that
    one; get_toc takes max_level (1-6) to leave out deeper headings and
    include_preview (false drops content_preview). Without them the answers
    are as before.
  - commit_reason_required (--commit-reason-required) now works: every tool
    that writes refuses a call without a non-empty reason and says so in its
    description. rename_page and delete_page take an optional reason (also
    the git commit message and passed to on_change handlers when given).
  - With --git, commits and history work against the real Git::Raw API:
    creating, updating, renaming, deleting and restoring a page are
    committed (a rename names both pages, a delete the removal), authored
    with the repository's git identity or "MCP::Wiki <mcp-wiki@localhost>";
    get_section_history and restore_section work, non-ASCII headings and
    text included, and get_section_history lists only the commits that
    created or changed the section. Before, every commit failed and the
    history tools could not read the repository.
  - With --git, a given reason is the commit message as is (no "Reason:"
    trailer); without one a message is generated, e.g. "Restore
    Intro#Alpha from 0123456" or "Renamed page: old -> new".
  - With --git, a wiki in a subdirectory of a git repository or in a linked
    worktree gets commits and history: the repository is found from the wiki
    root upwards and page names stay relative to the wiki root. If --git
    cannot be honoured (Git::Raw missing, no repository around the wiki),
    the server says so once at startup, without Perl's " at FILE line N.",
    and goes on without git, instead of warning on every write.
  - restore_section writes the restored page, not a section pushed into one
    heading; it keeps the page's final newline and the blank line before the
    next heading, and a page restored from a CRLF version gets no stray
    carriage returns. It refuses pages that are not valid UTF-8.
  - on_change events and the history tools name a page by its canonical path
    below the wiki root (as list_pages shows it), also when the client used
    ./, .. or an in-root symlink, so auto-commit stages the right file and
    history is found whatever the spelling; each handler gets its own copy
    of the event. bin/mcp-wiki no longer installs a handler that warned on
    every change.
  - Pages with CRLF line endings: get_paragraph returns the section with LF
    line endings, update_paragraph writes the page back with CRLF
    throughout, and an unchanged round trip stays byte-identical; TOC
    previews and char counts no longer count the CRs, and an empty "# "
    heading line no longer yields a heading named by a stray CR.
  - update_paragraph refuses pages that are not valid UTF-8 instead of
    replacing every non-UTF-8 byte with U+FFFD; the file stays untouched and
    reading such a page still works. Both update_paragraph and
    restore_section keep the page's file permissions (a 0600 page no longer
    becomes 0644) and refuse a page the server may not write.
  - get_paragraph and update_paragraph read the page once and work on that
    snapshot, so a write landing in between can no longer make an edit hit
    the wrong section. create_page and rename_page never replace a page that
    appeared between their existence check and the write.
  - Pages and directories with non-ASCII names work in every tool:
    list_pages returns them as text instead of double-encoded, pages below a
    non-ASCII directory are found again, error messages name them correctly,
    and names that are not valid UTF-8 are skipped with a warning.
    list_pages also skips a directory it cannot read (such as a root-only
    lost+found) with a warning instead of failing.
  - create_page without content starts the page with the file name without
    ".md" as its heading ("# c" for a/b/c.md), no longer the whole path.
  - Tool errors name the problem instead of an opaque "Internal error" (an
    unreadable page, a directory named like a page, a failing history lookup
    or restore), show no absolute paths or Perl source locations, and an
    unknown heading_path is answered with the page's heading paths (up to
    50).
  - get_toc and the section tools no longer slow down quadratically with the
    number of headings (8000 headings: about 42 s down to under 1 s).
  - Tool descriptions are complete: create_page declares its optional
    reason, heading_path is described as the full path from get_toc with an
    example, and every tool carries MCP annotations (read-only,
    destructive, idempotent hints). serverInfo reports the distribution's
    version instead of 1.0.0.
  - Docker: one image, raudssus/mcp-wiki on Docker Hub, built and pushed by
    the dzil bundle at release (the separate build-and-push script is gone).
    It is built from the built distribution, as dzil build does it or by
    hand with "dzil build" and "docker build" in the build directory, with
    its dependencies in a layer of its own; no dzil, cpanm or unused library
    goes into the image, it pins no PERL5LIB and exposes no port.
  - Docker: the image contains Git::Raw, so --git and the history tools work
    when the mounted directory is a git repository.
  - Docker: the working directory is /wiki, so the default wiki root is the
    mounted volume: "docker run -i --rm -v DIR:/wiki raudssus/mcp-wiki --git"
    serves DIR. The entrypoint checks the --wiki-root it was given (the last
    one), not just /wiki, and starts mcp-wiki without a shell in between, so
    a path or argument containing spaces or quotes arrives intact.
  - bin/mcp-wiki starts with "#!/usr/bin/env perl"; installs rewrite it to
    the installing perl as before.
  - The cpanfile matches the code: perl 5.020 is the declared minimum (what
    MCP and JSON::Schema::Tiny already require), namespace::clean is a
    runtime requirement, Git::Raw is recommended (history degrades cleanly
    without it); Markdown::Perl, Syntax::Keyword::Try, JSON::MaybeXS (now
    test only) and Digest::SHA (core) are no longer declared. t/05-cpanfile.t
    keeps it in sync.
  - Every module and bin/mcp-wiki carries its own $VERSION, the same as the
    distribution's. The server no longer needs the signatures feature.
  - MCP::Wiki::Git is a deprecated thin wrapper around
    MCP::Wiki::Git::History. Removed unused internals: Document
    update_content_hash and Git::History history_file (the
    .mcp-wiki-history.json was never read or written).
  - Docs: the README tool table lists the real arguments of every tool
    (heading_path, not heading) and shows the Docker usage; the mcp-wiki POD
    states what --git and --commit-reason-required do; every public method is
    documented in the POD along with the tool list; PLAN.md is marked as the
    original draft. t/06-readme-tools.t keeps README and POD in sync with the
    tool schemas and Getopt options.

0.001  2026-04-28
  - Initial release
  - MCP server for markdown wiki management
  - TOC extraction and paragraph-level editing
  - Git integration for history tracking